## Monthly Payment Gap Between 7.40% and 6.50% Mortgage Rates

At 7.40%, a 30-year fixed loan between $250,000 and $500,000 costs about $150 to $300 more a month in principal and interest than the same loan at 6.50%. Extra negotiating room in a buyer's market does not close that gap.

| Loan amount | Payment at 7.40% | Payment at 6.50% | Difference per month |
|---|---|---|---|
| $250,000 | $1,730.95 | $1,580.17 | $150.78 |
| $350,000 | $2,423.33 | $2,212.24 | $211.09 |
| $450,000 | $3,115.71 | $2,844.31 | $271.40 |
| $500,000 | $3,461.90 | $3,160.34 | $301.56 |

We computed each row with the standard amortization formula. The figures are illustrations and do not come from a lender quote. They leave out property taxes, homeowners insurance, and PMI.

The 7.40% rows use a week-old average. [Freddie Mac's PMMS](https://www.freddiemac.com/pmms) put the 30-year fixed at 7.32% as of October 22, 2026, down from 7.40% the prior week and up from 6.19% a year earlier. At 7.32%, a $400,000 loan costs $2,747.72 a month in principal and interest.

More metros favor buyers, yet a fixed budget buys less house. Realtor.com's Market Clock moved its national reading to 4 o'clock from 3 o'clock last quarter, which it describes as late balanced and loosening toward buyers, [Mortgage Professional America reports](https://www.mpamag.com/us/mortgage-industry/market-updates/more-markets-favor-buyers-so-why-does-affordability-keep-slipping/594082).

Of the 99 metros tracked, 17 are buyer's markets and 49 are balanced and loosening. Another 27 remain seller's markets, and 6 are balanced and tightening. The South holds 14 of the 17 buyer's markets.

Rates moved against buyers over the same stretch. They climbed 74 basis points in just over five weeks, from 6.66% in late August to 7.40% on October 8. For a buyer with a $2,000 monthly principal-and-interest budget, that rise removed more than $22,000 from the reachable home price, a 7.2% drop in purchasing power.

Realtor.com senior economist Jake Krimmel said "More buyers can negotiate in more markets." He added that with rising rates, leverage and purchasing power are moving in opposite directions. Chief economist Danielle Hale said rates above 7% limit what a fixed budget can buy.

Put the rate from your own quote into the [PITI calculator](/mortgage/piti-calculator/) and the [home affordability calculator](/mortgage/home-affordability-calculator/) before you set a price ceiling. Then negotiate on price and seller credits. Compare the rate itself on each lender's Loan Estimate, because a lower price cuts the loan amount but leaves the rate unchanged.

An adjustable-rate loan starts lower but resets. See the [fixed vs. ARM comparison](/compare/fixed-vs-arm-mortgage/) before you choose one. Discount points have their own break-even math in the points and rate locks section below.